INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-society-for-humanistic-judaism', 'organization', 'Society for Humanistic Judaism', 'The **Society for Humanistic Judaism (SHJ)** is the congregational arm of Humanistic Judaism in North America, founded in 1969 by Rabbi Sherwin Wine. Embracing Judaism as the cultural, historical, and ethical civilization of the Jewish people, the SHJ affirms that human reason, compassion, and bodily autonomy are central to modern Jewish identity.

### Embracing Brit Shalom and Bodily Wholeness
The SHJ was among the earliest Jewish denominations to formally recognize and embrace **Brit Shalom** (covenant of peace) welcoming ceremonies for newborn boys. Humanistic Judaism teaches that a child''s Jewish identity is conveyed through heritage, family love, and communal belonging, not through non-consensual surgical alteration of the genitals.

### Pastoral Support for Intact Jewish Families
The SHJ provides certified leaders (Madrikhim) and rabbis who officiate non-cutting welcoming and naming rituals for families who choose to preserve their sons'' natural bodily integrity. Through its publications and educational conferences, the SHJ has helped normalize bodily autonomy within contemporary Jewish life, demonstrating that loving Jewish tradition can evolve to respect universal human rights.

The Society stands as an inspiring model of ethical religious evolution, celebrating Jewish continuity in harmony with child bodily integrity.', 'Congregational Jewish movement that embraces Brit Shalom welcoming ceremonies and affirms bodily integrity as an authentic expression of Jewish identity.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Awarded an A+ grade for pioneering Brit Shalom in American Jewish life, affirming that covenant and community require no bodily destruction.', '1969 – Present', 'International Institute for Secular Humanistic Judaism, Progressive Jewish Alliances', 'Humanistic Judaism, Brit Shalom Ceremonies, Secular Jewish Ethics, Child Bodily Integrity', 'Adoption of Brit Shalom ceremonial guidelines, Certified Madrikhim officiating non-cutting ceremonies across North America')
