INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-george-wald', 'person', 'George Wald, Ph.D.', '**George Wald, Ph.D.** (1906–1997) was an internationally renowned American biochemist, Harvard University professor, and recipient of the 1967 **Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine** for his groundbreaking discoveries concerning the chemical and physiological visual processes in the eye. Beyond his scientific achievements, Wald was a passionate humanitarian, peace activist, and vocal champion of human rights.

### Academic Prestige in Defense of Bodily Integrity
In his later years, Dr. Wald lent his immense scientific authority to the emerging intactivist movement, actively supporting Marilyn Milos and the National Organization of Circumcision Information Resource Centers (NOCIRC). Wald spoke forcefully against routine infant circumcision, articulating that from the standpoint of evolutionary biology, every structure of the human body evolved for essential protective, sensory, and physiological purposes.

### Ethical Challenge to Medicine
Wald famously remarked on the absurdity of a medical establishment that routinely amputated healthy tissue from newborn boys, stating that humanity was not born with surgical defects requiring prophylactic correction. He addressed international symposia, urging physicians to honor the wisdom of natural biological design and adhere strictly to pediatric non-maleficence.

His vocal advocacy established an early precedent for Nobel laureates and elite scientists publicly aligning with the genital autonomy movement.', 'Nobel laureate in Physiology or Medicine (1967) and Harvard University biologist who lent his scientific prestige to opposing routine infant circumcision.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Awarded an A+ grade for lending Nobel-caliber scientific prestige to bodily integrity, affirming that the human prepuce is an evolutionary marvel, not a surgical defect.', '1932 – 1997', 'Harvard University, Nobel Foundation, NOCIRC Advisory Supporter', 'Biochemistry, Evolutionary Biology, Bioethics, Human Rights, Anti-War & Integrity Movements', 'Awarded Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine (1967), Keynote address supporting international symposia on genital autonomy')
