INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-joseph-lewis', 'person', 'Joseph Lewis', '**Joseph Lewis** (1889–1968) was an American freethought activist, publisher, and author who served as president of the Freethinkers of America. In 1949, Lewis published *In the Name of Humanity*, a groundbreaking 158-page treatise that provided the first comprehensive secular, medical, and ethical critique of routine infant male circumcision published in 20th-century America.

### ''In the Name of Humanity'' (1949)
Published by the Freethought Press Association, Lewis''s book arrived at the precise moment American post-WWII hospital birth routines were cementing infant circumcision as a cultural default. Lewis analyzed the devastating trauma of surgery without anesthesia, documented fatal hemorrhages and surgical amputations, and exposed the pseudoscientific arguments used by hospitals to justify the practice. He distributed thousands of copies directly to physicians, judges, and expectant parents across the nation.

### A Forgotten Prophet of Bodily Integrity
Lewis framed the intact body as a fundamental human right, arguing that no state, religion, or medical association holds the right to alter the body of an infant without consent. Decades ahead of his time, he warned that America was embarking on a mass surgical experiment that future generations would look back upon with profound moral horror.

Lewis''s seminal book remains an irreplaceable historical milestone in the pre-NOCIRC era of bodily autonomy advocacy.', 'American author whose 1949 treatise ''In the Name of Humanity'' provided the first comprehensive modern critique of routine infant circumcision.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Awarded an A+ grade for authoring ''In the Name of Humanity'' in 1949, bravely sounding the alarm against the institutionalization of infant circumcision in America.', '1920 – 1968', 'Freethinkers of America (President), Freethought Press Association', 'Freethought, Secular Ethics, Child Protection, Historical Critique of Infant Circumcision', 'Publication of ''In the Name of Humanity'' (1949), National educational campaigns distributing treatises to American physicians')
