INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-herbert-snow', 'person', 'Herbert Snow, M.D.', '**Herbert Lumley Snow, M.D.** (1847–1930) was an eminent British surgeon, cancer specialist, and author who served for nearly thirty years as senior surgeon at the London Cancer Hospital (now the Royal Marsden Hospital). In the late 19th century, as the Victorian medical establishment began enthusiastically adopting routine circumcision, Dr. Snow emerged as one of the few prominent surgical authorities to fiercely denounce the practice as unscientific and barbaric.

### Debunking Victorian Pathologization
In his 1890 clinical treatises and medical articles, Dr. Snow systematically debunked claims made by surgeons like Lewis Sayre that the foreskin caused reflex neurosis, epilepsy, hip joint disease, or penile cancer. Snow demonstrated that penile cancer was an exceptionally rare disease of late adulthood tied to gross neglect, not a justification for amputating the healthy tissue of all newborn infants.

### The ''Barbaric Mutilation'' Critique
Snow publicly attacked routine circumcision as a ''useless, unnecessary, and cruel mutilation,'' warning that Victorian physicians were manufacturing pathologies to justify surgical fees and satisfy puritanical moral panics over masturbation. His courageous opposition placed him at odds with the surgical mainstream of his era.

Dr. Snow''s writings stand as a vital historical testament proving that the medical critique of circumcision did not originate with modern intactivism, but was voiced by leading surgical authorities over a century ago.', '19th-century British surgeon at London Cancer Hospital who early challenged Victorian pathologization of the prepuce and denounced routine circumcision.', 'champion', 0, 5, 'A', 'Awarded an A grade for courageous 19th-century surgical dissent, exposing Victorian circumcision rationalizations as cruel, profit-driven fallacies.', '1875 – 1910', 'London Cancer Hospital (Royal Marsden), Royal College of Surgeons', 'Surgical Oncology, 19th-Century Medical Reform, Critique of Reflex Neurosis, Penile Cancer Epidemiology', 'Published seminal critiques of routine circumcision (1890), Senior Surgeon at London Cancer Hospital for 29 years')
