INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-chpw', 'organization', 'Community Health Plan of Washington (CHPW)', 'The **Community Health Plan of Washington (CHPW)** is a not-for-profit Medicaid managed care organization founded in 1992 by Washington State''s Community and Migrant Health Centers. Serving hundreds of thousands of low-income Washington residents enrolled in Apple Health (Medicaid), CHPW is cited in *The Accidental Intactivist''s Manifesto* as a prime example of institutional normalization and public subsidization of routine infant circumcision.

### Taxpayer-Funded ''Benefits+'' Normalization
Under its ''Benefits+'' program, CHPW markets non-therapeutic neonatal circumcision to enrolled mothers as a covered, cost-free perk alongside genuine preventive health measures like vision exams, gym memberships, and prenatal vitamins. By packaging an elective, irreversible surgical excision of healthy erogenous tissue as a routine ''benefit'', CHPW removes financial friction for parents and subtly signals that the procedure is medically recommended.

### Policy Inconsistency and Legislative Reform
While Washington State Medicaid policies are theoretically designed to fund medically necessary healthcare, health plans like CHPW continue to reimburse hospital and outpatient circumcision claims under diagnostic catch-all codes. Advocacy groups and policy analysts have pointed to CHPW as a key example of how public healthcare funds are diverted from vital maternal-child care to subsidize cosmetic infant surgeries.

Efforts by policy advocates in Washington aim to realign Medicaid spending with evidence-based medicine by eliminating public reimbursement for non-therapeutic infant circumcision.', 'Washington State Medicaid managed care health plan illustrating the institutional normalization and taxpayer subsidization of elective infant circumcision.', 'critic', 0, 2, 'D', 'Assigned a D grade for packaging elective infant circumcision as a ''free benefit'', improperly spending taxpayer Medicaid dollars on non-therapeutic amputation.', '1992 – Present', 'Washington Apple Health (Medicaid), Community Health Network of Washington', 'Medicaid Administration, Managed Care Reimbursement, Elective Surgery Subsidies, Healthcare Policy', 'Inclusion of neonatal circumcision in ''Benefits+'' package, Target of state Medicaid funding reform campaigns')
