INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-lord-templeman', 'person', 'Lord Sydney Templeman', '**Lord Sydney Templeman** (1920–2014) was a distinguished British judge who served as a Lord of Appeal in Ordinary (Law Lord) in the House of Lords from 1982 to 1994. Known for his robust moral clarity and influential common law judgments, Lord Templeman authored the leading majority opinion in the landmark 1993 House of Lords case *R v Brown*.

### The Principle of Bodily Harm and Consent
In *R v Brown*, the House of Lords established the definitive common law rule governing consent and physical injury: an individual cannot lawfully consent to the infliction of actual bodily harm unless the activity falls within a recognized, legally justified exception (such as lawful surgery, sports, or tattooing). Lord Templeman famously held that society is entitled to protect individuals against the cult of violence and that consent alone does not sanitize bodily violation.

### Application to Pediatric Surgery & Intactivism
While *R v Brown* concerned consensual adult sadomasochism, Templeman''s legal reasoning has become a central touchstone in British and Commonwealth bioethics regarding non-therapeutic infant circumcision. Legal scholars and organizations (including 15Square and Attorneys for the Rights of the Child) emphasize that if competent adults cannot consent to non-therapeutic bodily harm, parents cannot legally grant proxy consent for the permanent surgical amputation of healthy genital tissue from an infant incapable of consent.

Templeman''s jurisprudence remains foundational in international debates defining the limits of surgical consent and state protection of physical integrity.', 'British Law Lord whose majority opinion in R v Brown (1993) established that consent cannot legalize bodily harm without recognized social justification.', 'notable', 0, 4, 'B+', 'Recognized with a B+ grade for articulating the common law principle that consent cannot sanitize non-therapeutic bodily harm, a pillar of bodily integrity law.', '1960 – 1994', 'House of Lords (Law Lord), High Court of Justice, Court of Appeal of England and Wales', 'English Common Law, Consent Doctrine, Limits of Parental Authority, Criminal Battery Precedents', 'Authored leading House of Lords opinion in R v Brown [1993] 2 All ER 75, Foundational citation in child bodily integrity jurisprudence')
