INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-david-reimer', 'person', 'David Reimer', '**David Reimer** (1965–2004) was a Canadian man whose tragic life following a botched infancy circumcision became one of the most famous and consequential cases in the history of medicine, psychiatry, and sexology (the ''John/Joan'' case).

### The 1966 Surgical Disaster
At eight months old, David (then Bruce) and his twin brother Brian were taken to a local hospital in Winnipeg to treat minor phimosis. The surgeon utilized an experimental electrocautery device that completely destroyed David''s penis. Desperate for a solution, his parents contacted Dr. John Money, a prominent psychologist at Johns Hopkins University who pioneered theories claiming that gender identity was entirely socially constructed.

### The Money Experiment and Reimer''s Reclamation
Under Money''s direction, David was surgically castrated at 22 months, administered estrogen, and raised as a girl named Brenda. For decades, Money published triumphant academic papers citing the case as proof that gender identity was purely nurture. In reality, David experienced intense gender dysphoria, profound depression, and persistent identity conflict throughout childhood. At age 14, upon learning the truth from his father, he immediately transitioned back to male, underwent surgical reconstruction, and took the name David.

### Dismantling Pediatric Dogma
In 1997, Dr. Milton Diamond published an independent follow-up exposing Money''s deception, followed by journalist John Colapinto''s bestselling 2000 biography *As Nature Made Him: The Boy Who Was Raised as a Girl*. David went public to ensure no other child would suffer similar experimental mutilation. His tragedy conclusively demonstrated that human gender and genital identity are innate, biological, and cannot be socially engineered, delivering a permanent blow to pediatric non-consensual genital modifications.', 'Canadian man (1965–2004) whose life following a botched infancy circumcision disproved John Money''s social gender theories and exposed surgical abuse.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Honored with an A+ grade for his immense courage in publicly exposing surgical experimentation, establishing that bodily and gender identity are biologically innate.', '1965 – 2004', 'John/Joan Case, Dr. Milton Diamond Collaborator, As Nature Made Him (Subject)', 'Surgical Complications, Innate Gender Identity, Pediatric Bioethics, Non-Consensual Genital Surgery', '1966 Electrocautery Incident, Reclaimed Male Identity at 14 (1979), Diamond Academic Exposé (1997), Publication of ''As Nature Made Him'' (2000)')
