INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-dan-savage', 'person', 'Dan Savage', '**Dan Savage** is an American author, media pundit, journalist, and internationally syndicated sex-advice columnist (*Savage Love*). Widely recognized for his frank, humor-infused, and ethics-centered approach to human sexuality, Savage has been a prominent cultural commentator on relationships, parenting, and bodily autonomy for over three decades.

### ''The Kid'' and the Parental Awakening
In his acclaimed 1999 memoir *The Kid: What Happened After My Boyfriend and I Decided to Go Get Pregnant*, Savage chronicled the adoption of his son with his partner Terry Miller. A central narrative arc in the book details their unexpected journey grappling with the question of neonatal circumcision. Initially assuming they would follow cultural defaults, Savage undertook exhaustive research into medical evidence, anatomical function, and international norms, ultimately deciding to leave his son intact.

### The ''Price of Admission'' & Cultural Normalization
Savage''s personal account became an influential touchstone for modern parents, illustrating how questioning the societal autopilot requires conscious reflection. His articulation of the cultural pressures and irrational rationalizations surrounding circumcision helped normalize the choice of intactness within progressive and mainstream parenting circles.

Across his columns, podcasts, and speaking appearances, Savage has continued to speak critically of routine non-therapeutic circumcision, framing it as an unnecessary cosmetic modification that robs children of sexual erogenous tissue before they can grant consent.', 'Syndicated advice columnist and author whose memoir ''The Kid'' provided an influential mainstream narrative on choosing intactness for one''s child.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A', 'Awarded an A grade for candidly documenting his parental journey in ''The Kid'', helping dismantle cultural autopilot and making intactness mainstream and celebrated.', '1991 – Present', 'Savage Love, The Stranger, It Gets Better Project', 'Sexology, Parental Autonomy, Cultural Deconstruction, LGBTQ+ Advocacy, Mainstream Media', 'Publication of ''The Kid'' (1999), Co-founding of It Gets Better Project (2010), Decades of syndicated advocacy for bodily integrity')
