INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-morris-sorrells', 'person', 'Morris L. Sorrells, M.D.', '**Morris L. Sorrells, M.D.** is an American physician, clinical researcher, and landmark champion in penile somatosensory neurology. He is the lead author of the seminal 2007 quantitative sensory study *Fine-touch pressure thresholds in the adult penis*, published in *BJU International*.

### The Semmes-Weinstein Fine-Touch Mapping
Prior to Dr. Sorrells''s 2007 study, claims that circumcision did not impair penile sensation relied almost exclusively on an unverified, non-peer-reviewed assertion in Masters & Johnson''s 1966 book. Dr. Sorrells designed a rigorous, controlled clinical protocol utilizing individually calibrated Semmes-Weinstein monofilaments across 19 specific anatomical sites on the penis (controlled for age, underwear type, time since ejaculation, and ethnicity).

### Core Empirical Findings
Sorrells and his team demonstrated that:
1. The glans of circumcised men is significantly less sensitive to fine touch than that of intact men (p = 0.040).
2. The transitional region from external to internal prepuce (the ridged band) is the most sensitive region of the intact penis, more sensitive than the most sensitive site on the circumcised penis.
3. Five specific preputial locations routinely amputated during circumcision had significantly lower pressure thresholds (i.e. far greater fine-touch sensitivity) than the ventral scar of the circumcised penis.

His research remains the foundational evidentiary bedrock dismantling the medical myth that the foreskin is ''functionless'' or that circumcision preserves full penile sensitivity.', 'Physician and lead author of the landmark 2007 BJU International sensory mapping study proving that circumcision ablates the most sensitive parts of the human penis.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Awarded an A+ grade for executing the definitive quantitative sensory investigation that conclusively proved circumcision ablates the most fine-touch sensitive tissues of the adult penis.', '2000 – Present', 'BJU International, NOCIRC, San Francisco Medical Community', 'Penile Somatosensory Neurology, Semmes-Weinstein Monofilament Testing, Fine-Touch Pressure Thresholds, Foreskin Sensation', 'Publication of ''Fine-touch pressure thresholds in the adult penis'' in BJU International (2007), Western Institutional Review Board Sensory Protocol, International Symposia Keynote')
