INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-kamyar-fendereski', 'person', 'Kamyar Fendereski, M.D.', '**Kamyar Fendereski, M.D.** is a physician and surgical epidemiologist affiliated with the Department of Surgery at the University of Utah School of Medicine. He is the lead investigator of the massive 2024 commercial claims cohort study *Comparing Penile Problems in Circumcised vs. Uncircumcised Boys: Insights From a Large Commercial Claims Database*, published in the *Journal of Pediatric Surgery*.

### The 1.7-Million Boy Claims Cohort
Addressing decades of conflicting, small-sample retrospective studies, Dr. Fendereski analyzed the MarketScan commercial claims database, tracking 850,000 circumcised boys matched 1-to-1 with 850,000 intact control subjects over a 10-year period through age 5. The primary endpoint was documented penile medical complications requiring clinical encounter (meatal stenosis, urethral stricture, post-operative adhesions, and penile inflammation).

### Revolutionary Findings
The investigation established that circumcised boys suffered a 1.7% incidence of penile medical complications compared to just 0.5% in intact boys, representing an age- and multivariable-adjusted **2.9-fold higher risk (95% CI 2.8–3.1, p < 0.001)** for circumcised boys.

This landmark paper directly refuted the core historical claim of the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP 2012) that neonatal circumcision reduces cumulative penile medical problems in early childhood.', 'University of Utah surgical researcher whose 1.7-million boy cohort study proved circumcised boys suffer 2.9 times more penile medical complications through age 5.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Awarded an A+ grade for spearheading the largest matched cohort study in pediatric surgical history, empirically demonstrating that neonatal circumcision triples complication rates in early childhood.', '2020 – Present', 'University of Utah School of Medicine, Division of Urology, Journal of Pediatric Surgery', 'Pediatric Surgical Epidemiology, Commercial Claims Databases, Penile Complications, Meatal Stenosis, Health Services Research', 'Lead author of 1.7M claims cohort study in Journal of Pediatric Surgery (2024), Disproved prophylactic hygiene claims of routine infant circumcision')
