INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-5360914a', 'person', 'Edgar J. Schoen', 'Edgar J. Schoen, M.D., was an American pediatrician whose institutional defense of infant male circumcision positioned him as one of the most prominent critics of the genital autonomy and intactivism movement. Within the historical discourse of pediatric medicine and bodily integrity, Schoen is widely cited for his leadership in shaping institutional policy, notably through his documented involvement with the *Report of the Task Force on Circumcision*. Throughout his career, he frequently clashed with human rights advocates and reform-minded clinicians who contested the ethical, physical, and psychological legitimacy of non-therapeutic neonatal surgery.

Across intactivist scholarship, Schoen’s work serves as a primary ideological counterpoint examined by researchers and bioethicists. His perspectives regarding procedural pain and clinical rationales are scrutinized in critical analyses such as *Babies Don''t Feel Pain: A Century of Denial in Medicine* and the ethical critique *Justifying the Unjustifiable: Rite v. Wrong*. Furthermore, his defense of routine neonatal circumcision is directly challenged by early experiential research within the movement, including *AWAKENINGS: A Preliminary Poll of Circumcised Men Revealing the Long-Term Harm and Healing the Wounds of Infant Circumcision*, which contrasts institutional medical justifications with the long-term physical and psychological harms reported by affected men. Within the movement''s archives, Schoen remains a defining figure of traditional pediatric opposition to genital autonomy.', 'Prominent American pediatrician and vocal advocate for routine neonatal circumcision who chaired the American Academy of Pediatrics'' 1989 Task Force on Circumcision.', 'critic', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
