INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-c96bb47e', 'person', 'Thomas E. Wiswell', '**Thomas E. Wiswell** is an American pediatrician, neonatologist, and medical researcher who emerged as a central figure in late-twentieth-century debates over neonatal circumcision. Within the history of medicine and the genital autonomy movement, Wiswell is primarily recognized as a prominent critic of the intact status, having conducted extensive research asserting a direct correlation between the intact prepuce and an increased incidence of infantile urinary tract infections (UTIs). His findings significantly shaped organized medicine''s deliberations, directly informing major institutional documents such as the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) *Report of the Task Force on Circumcision* as well as its policy statements addressing circumcision and urinary tract infection. His perspectives and shifting clinical recommendations were further detailed in scholarly contributions such as *Neonatal circumcision revisited*.

Within the context of intactivism and the global movement for bodily integrity, Wiswell’s body of work represents one of the most frequently challenged clinical justifications for prophylactic genital alteration. Human rights advocates, medical ethicists, and interdisciplinary researchers have extensively analyzed his statistical associations and prophylactic rationale, critically addressing his conclusions in works such as *Justifying the Unjustifiable: Rite v. Wrong*. Furthermore, evolutionary and anatomical critiques—exemplified by texts such as *De Virginibus Puerisque: The Function of the Human Foreskin Considered from an Evolutionary Perspective*—situate Wiswell’s clinical framework against the physiological, immunological, and protective functions of the natural foreskin, maintaining his position as a key point of reference in ongoing discussions regarding medical ethics and non-therapeutic infant surgery.', 'A prominent pediatrician and researcher whose 1980s studies on urinary tract infections significantly influenced American pediatric policy in favor of routine infant circumcision.', 'critic', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
