INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-1bdd58a1', 'person', 'Andrew Freedman', '**Andrew Freedman**, MD, is an American pediatric urologist who has figured prominently in modern medical and ethical discourses surrounding non-therapeutic neonatal circumcision. Within the broader history of the genital autonomy and intactivism movements, Freedman is primarily documented through his work as a member of the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) Task Force on Circumcision, where he contributed significantly to the drafting of the influential 2012 *Circumcision Policy Statement* and its accompanying *Technical Report: Male Circumcision*. 

Freedman’s prominence in archival literature stems from his role as an institutional defender of the procedure and a vocal critic of the arguments posed by intactivist and human rights organizations. In the wake of widespread international dissent from bioethicists and European medical associations challenging the AAP’s stance, Freedman co-authored the rebuttal, "Cultural Bias and Circumcision: The AAP Task Force on Circumcision Responds," defending the methodological validity and cultural neutrality of the task force’s findings. His contributions and perspectives have continued to be examined across academic and journalistic analyses—including pieces such as "Fleshing Out Change On Circumcision" and discussions surrounding the need to rethink circumcision policy as public controversies intensify—solidifying his status as a key interlocutor in the ongoing global debate over infant bodily integrity and parental decision-making.', 'Pediatric urologist and former vice-chair of the AAP Task Force on Circumcision who co-authored the 2012 AAP policy statement.', 'critic', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
