INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-bc1ff852', 'organization', 'World Health Organization (WHO)', 'The World Health Organization (WHO), established in 1948 as a specialized agency of the United Nations, is responsible for directing and coordinating international public health. Headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland, the agency focuses on establishing global health standards, formulating evidence-based policy options, and monitoring worldwide health trends. Because of its institutional authority, the WHO’s declarations, definitions, and health frameworks carry immense weight in global health governance and human rights discourse.

Within the literature of genital autonomy and the broader intactivist movement, the WHO functions primarily as a central institutional benchmark and frequent subject of critical evaluation. Scholars and advocates analyzing the bodily integrity of minors regularly examine the WHO’s policies, definitions, and public health directives concerning non-therapeutic genital cutting. The organization features prominently across legal, medical, social, and theological scholarship—such as comparative analyses of male and female circumcision and examinations of the religious and cultural legitimization of genital cutting. 

Additionally, the WHO''s stances are frequently scrutinized in regional human rights debates, including legal and ethical discussions surrounding ritual infant male circumcision in Denmark, as well as phenomenological investigations into traditional practices in Iran. In these analytical frameworks, the agency often serves as a focal point for critics and legal scholars who assess the consistency of international public health mandates against universal standards of bodily integrity, medical ethics, and children''s rights.', 'Specialized United Nations agency whose global health policy endorsing medical male circumcision for HIV prevention became a central subject of international bioethical debate.', 'critic', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
