INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-8870f1fd', 'person', 'Sigmund Freud', '**Sigmund Freud** (1856–1939), the Austrian neurologist and founder of psychoanalysis, occupies a prominent theoretical position within the historical, psychological, and cultural scholarship of the genital autonomy movement. Intactivist literature frequently engages Freud’s foundational frameworks concerning psychosexual development, unconscious symbolism, and castration anxiety to investigate the root causes, cultural transmission, and psychological sequelae of non-therapeutic genital modifications.

Within the broader movement for bodily integrity, Freud’s concepts are extensively utilized to critique both ritual and clinical cutting practices. His psychoanalytic model informs analyses of religious and theological texts—such as investigations into circumcision as a form of regressive conversion within Pauline epistolary traditions—as well as scholarly evaluations of Western discourses on sexuality. Furthermore, human rights and psychological frameworks addressing forced genital cutting of children draw upon Freudian paradigms to examine the unresolved trauma, ethical dilemmas, and emotional fallout experienced by affected individuals, highlighting the tension between bodily autonomy and cultural compulsion.

Freud’s theoretical influence also intersects with the gendered and cross-cultural analyses compiled in the archive. His writings provided early intellectual touchstones for the masculine foundational literature of the intactivist movement, which sought to deconstruct cultural assumptions surrounding male vulnerability, sexuality, and bodily integrity. Additionally, his work remains a point of comparative reference in medical, psychological, and economic evaluations of circumcision across various regional contexts, including the Middle East and Iran, positioning psychoanalytic theory as an enduring lens through which advocates and researchers critique non-consensual genital procedures.', 'Austrian neurologist and founder of psychoanalysis whose theories famously linked circumcision to castration anxiety and unconscious psychological trauma.', 'notable', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
