INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-e17343a6', 'organization', 'Al-Azhar University', '**Al-Azhar University**, located in Cairo, Egypt, is globally recognized as one of the oldest and most preeminent centers of Islamic scholarship, jurisprudence, and higher learning. Within the broader discourse of genital autonomy and the human rights movement against non-therapeutic cutting, the institution occupies a notable and complex position that bridges theological doctrine and clinical medical research. The university’s theological output and legal rulings are frequently cited in scholarly examinations of religious tradition, such as *Islamic Law and the Issue of Male and Female Circumcision* and *To Mutilate in the Name of Jehovah or Allah: Legitimization of Male and Female Circumcision*, which critically evaluate the historical, theological, and institutional justifications surrounding both male and female genital cutting.

Concurrently, medical researchers and clinicians affiliated with Al-Azhar contribute vital empirical literature to the field of pediatric surgery and bodily integrity, specifically regarding the diagnosis and mitigation of severe iatrogenic complications. Academic and clinical reports associated with the university have documented critical postoperative pathologies, including the management of penile post-circumcision ischemia through pentoxifylline infusion and hyperbaric oxygen therapy. Furthermore, clinical investigations by its medical teams have illuminated acute vascular traumas in children—such as phlebectasia and phlebothrombosis of the penile veins following non-medical circumcisions—providing the global movement for genital integrity with crucial clinical evidence regarding the physical risks and adverse health consequences associated with neonatal and pediatric genital modifications.', 'A historic Islamic university in Cairo whose religious decrees heavily influence legal, public health, and human rights discourses on circumcision and female genital cutting.', 'notable', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
