INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-b4f0c923', 'organization', 'World Health Organisation', 'The World Health Organization (WHO), established in 1948 as a specialized agency of the United Nations, is tasked with directing and coordinating international public health. Headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland, the organization monitors health trends, establishes international health norms and standards, and articulates evidence-based health policies across its member states. Within the intellectual and bioethical framework of genital autonomy and intactivism, the WHO holds a prominent and complex position, functioning simultaneously as a formal critic of certain forms of non-therapeutic cutting while drawing rigorous scrutiny from scholars for its endorsements of others.

In the sphere of female genital cutting, the WHO acts as a leading critic and regulatory authority. The organization defines, categorizes, and unequivocally condemns female genital mutilation (FGM) as a fundamental violation of human rights, bodily autonomy, and medical ethics. Intactivist legal and human rights literature frequently analyzes the WHO’s anti-FGM stances, often referencing them to contextualize state and international protection laws or to highlight inconsistencies in child protection policies where the legal prohibition of female procedures contrasts starkly with the legal tolerance of male infant cutting.

Conversely, the WHO’s public health policies regarding male circumcision have situated it at the center of intense debate. Following its 2007 recommendations endorsing male circumcision as an intervention against HIV transmission, the WHO’s policies have been examined through historical, cultural, and ethical lenses. Critical analyses within the archive assess the social representations of WHO-backed circumcision campaigns as HIV prophylaxis—such as in Zimbabwe—and draw critical parallels between modern HIV policies and nineteenth-century medical tendencies to treat healthy human anatomy as a vector for disease. Furthermore, bioethicists challenge the application of these public health directives to non-therapeutic infant circumcision, arguing that such measures bypass the autonomy of pediatric patients who cannot provide informed consent. Consequently, the WHO remains one of the most significant institutional reference points in contemporary scholarly debates surrounding genital integrity and medical necessity.', 'Specialized United Nations public health agency whose promotion of medical male circumcision for HIV prevention made it a primary target of global intactivist critique.', 'critic', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
