INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-aec51975', 'person', 'Judith Butler', 'Judith Butler (born February 24, 1956) is an influential American philosopher, gender theorist, and academic whose conceptual frameworks have profoundly shaped the discourse surrounding bodily integrity, human rights, and the social construction of the body. Holding the position of Maxine Elliot Professor in the Department of Comparative Literature and the Program of Critical Theory at the University of California, Berkeley, Butler is internationally recognized for pioneering the theory of gender performativity and interrogating the regulatory mechanisms through which physical bodies are gendered, normalized, and politicized.

Within the broader movement for genital autonomy, Butler’s theoretical corpus serves as an essential analytical foundation for critiquing non-therapeutic genital modifications. Scholars and autonomy advocates draw heavily upon Butler''s work to deconstruct the Western sex/gender binary and to examine how bodily practices—ranging from infant male circumcision to female genital mutilation/cutting (FGM/C) and emergent surgical technologies—are deployed to enforce normative gender identities. Butler''s theories of physical vulnerability and sovereign bodily boundaries are particularly central to discussions concerning the intersections of secularism, religious freedom, and bodily integrity, as well as analyses of how ethnic belonging and cultural identity are inscribed upon the flesh.

Butler’s conceptual contributions frequently anchor key scholarly texts examining the ethics of genital cutting, notably informing critical inquiries into the sociocultural, psychoanalytic, and legal narratives that justify involuntary alteration. By providing critical tools to dissect how bodily vulnerability and normative sexuality are framed in the United States and globally, Butler’s philosophy remains a vital intellectual resource for advancing universal standards of somatic self-determination.', 'An influential philosopher and gender theorist whose foundational concepts of bodily autonomy, performativity, and physical vulnerability inform scholarly critiques of non-consensual body modification.', 'notable', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
