INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-5066723c', 'person', 'John Money', '**John William Money** (1921–2006) was a New Zealand-American psychologist, sexologist, and author who served as a professor of pediatrics and medical psychology at Johns Hopkins University. Renowned for his early research into the development of gender identity and the clinical management of variations of sex characteristics (intersex traits), Money was instrumental in formulating clinical protocols that advocated for the early surgical normalization and assignment of infants born with atypical genitalia, based on his hypothesis that gender role development was largely malleable through environmental conditioning.

Within the context of intactivism and the global movement for genital autonomy, Money’s theories and clinical legacy represent a critical focal point of bioethical and human rights scrutiny. Rather than being seen as a proponent of bodily integrity, his protocols regarding intersex genital interventions are extensively analyzed in relation to the broader framework of non-consensual childhood genital modifications, including male circumcision and female genital cutting. Scholarly discourse examining the policing of gender and the human rights of children frequently positions Money''s historical practices as foundational examples of medicalized body alteration that subordinated a child’s right to bodily integrity to social and aesthetic norms.

Furthermore, literature concerning the trauma, psychosexual outcomes, and ethical violations associated with infant genital alterations regularly cites Money''s clinical outcomes. In evaluations of children''s rights across comparative international studies and bioethical assessments of non-therapeutic surgeries, Money’s work is critically referenced to illustrate the long-term harms of preemptive, non-consensual interventions performed on the genitalia of healthy or anatomically atypical children.', 'Sexologist whose controversial theories and management of the David Reimer case made him a focal point in critiques of non-consensual infant genital surgery.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
