INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-d0491d0b', 'person', 'Isaac Baker Brown', '**Isaac Baker Brown** (1812–1873) was a nineteenth-century English gynecologist and surgeon whose controversial medical career serves as a significant historical touchstone within the historiography of genital autonomy. Operating during the Victorian era, Brown achieved prominence—and eventual professional ostracization—for his aggressive promotion of clitoridectomy and related sexual surgeries, which he advocated as clinical cures for hysteria, epilepsy, and behavioral disorders attributed to the nineteenth-century masturbation taboo. 

Within the human rights and intactivist archives, Brown is frequently cited in historical analyses tracing the rise of sexual surgery and Western medical discourses surrounding bodily modification. Scholarly examinations of late-nineteenth-century medical practices in Britain and America highlight Brown’s work to illustrate how Victorian anxieties over adolescent sexuality and masturbation provided the initial justification for surgical interventions on the genitalia of both sexes, laying early ideological groundwork that intersected with the subsequent normalization of routine male circumcision.

Brown’s legacy is particularly central to modern comparative ethics regarding male and female genital cutting. Scholars and critics within the movement for genital autonomy examine his career to interrogate the intellectual inconsistencies and historical double standards that arose when Western medicine decisively rejected female genital alterations like Brown''s while simultaneously institutionalizing non-therapeutic male circumcision. Consequently, Brown’s clinical history remains a critical focal point in discussions addressing the shared roots, similarities, and divergent cultural framing of involuntary genital cutting.', '19th-century English surgeon infamous for promoting non-consensual clitoridectomies as a cure for female mental illness.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
