INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-f594bf6e', 'person', 'Philo of Alexandria', '**Philo of Alexandria** (c. 20 BCE – c. 50 CE) was a prominent Hellenistic Jewish philosopher and statesman who lived in the intellectual hub of Alexandria, Roman Egypt. Bridging the traditions of classical Greek philosophy—primarily Platonism and Stoicism—with biblical exegesis, Philo sought to articulate Jewish law and scripture through rationalist and allegorical frameworks. His extensive writings represent some of the earliest preserved philosophical treatments of biblical mandates, directly influencing both Hellenistic Judaism and early Christian theology.

Within the archival history of genital autonomy and the broader critique of non-therapeutic bodily modifications, Philo serves as a pivotal historical figure in debates over identity, gender, and power. Scholars analyzing the historical trajectory of circumcision—specifically interpretations of the Abrahamic covenant in Genesis 17:1–14—frequently position Philo alongside thinkers like Origen and the early rabbis. His work is critically examined in studies regarding "marked and unmarked flesh," illuminating how physical alterations were rationalized, codified, and differentiated from internalized spiritual ethics, such as the philosophical metaphor of circumcision being purely a "matter of the heart."

Philo’s treatises are equally significant for documenting the forgotten critics of circumcision in antiquity. In addressing contemporaries who argued against the physical procedure in favor of strictly allegorical or ethical interpretations, Philo engaged deeply with early internal skepticism surrounding the practice. Although Philo himself defended the physical observance while simultaneously expounding its symbolic meanings, his texts remain an indispensable record of early Jewish critiques, controversies, and counter-arguments concerning bodily integrity and the imposition of communal identity upon the flesh.', 'Hellenistic Jewish philosopher who authored one of antiquity''s foundational philosophical defenses of circumcision.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
