INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-bf8c9f35', 'person', 'Fran Hosken', '**Fran Hosken** (1919–2006) was an Austrian-born American author, architect, feminist, and human rights champion who played a foundational role in initiating the international campaign against female genital cutting. As an early vanguard in the broader movement for bodily integrity and genital autonomy, Hosken was among the first to bring widespread global visibility to the practice, documenting its prevalence, public health impacts, and socio-cultural dimensions while vigorously framing non-therapeutic genital alteration as a violation of fundamental human rights. 

Within the archival literature on genital autonomy, Hosken’s body of work is a seminal reference point for the intersection of female genital cutting and its profound implications for female sexuality. Her pioneering efforts feature prominently in critical anthropological and sociological analyses examining the development of Western discourses on sexuality and the comparative dynamics between the Global North and South. Academic scholarship regularly situates her advocacy alongside modern inquiries into gendered genital modifications, spanning from foundational campaigns against traditional cutting practices to newer anatomical technologies within the contemporary sex and gender system. 

Additionally, Hosken’s legacy informs ethnographic research on cultural shifts across diaspora communities, including studies investigating how exiled populations—such as Somalis in Sweden—reassess and abandon traditional circumcision practices when navigating Western socio-cultural environments. Her documentation remains an enduring cornerstone in the history of international activism for genital autonomy, serving both as an impetus for policy reform and a subject of critical analysis regarding how cross-cultural human rights advocacy is articulated and received.', 'Feminist author, publisher, and pioneer in international advocacy against female genital mutilation', 'champion', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
