INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-be709441', 'person', 'David Gollaher', 'David L. Gollaher is an American medical historian and bioethicist whose scholarly documentation of circumcision has become a cornerstone reference within the global movement for genital autonomy. Through his extensive research into the cultural, clinical, and sociological history of surgical practices, Gollaher has provided human rights advocates and legal scholars with critical historical context regarding how non-therapeutic neonatal circumcision transitioned from a religious rite into a routine Western medical procedure.

Within the literature of bodily integrity, Gollaher’s work is prominently cited for exposing the Victorian-era medicalization of the foreskin. His historical scholarship illuminates how late-nineteenth-century medical authorities pathologized normal anatomy, utilizing circumcision as a prophylactic measure and a purported cure for masturbation, hysteria, and various systemic ailments. His analyses of fluctuating neonatal circumcision ratios across the twentieth century have helped researchers track cultural shifts, shedding light on the institutional momentum that sustained the procedure long after its original medical justifications were disproven.

Gollaher’s critical assessment of the practice continues to influence modern bioethical and psychological frameworks. His scholarship is frequently drawn upon by modern scholars examining non-therapeutic infant circumcision as an ethical violation against non-consenting individuals, as well as those assessing childhood genital cutting through the lens of adverse childhood experiences (ACEs). By cataloging the shifting rationales of what has been characterized as the world''s most controversial surgery, Gollaher occupies a notable role as an essential chronicler of the medical and societal attitudes that the intactivist movement seeks to reform.', 'Medical historian and author of ''Circumcision: A History of the World''s Most Controversial Operation.''', 'notable', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
