INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-b0206dee', 'person', 'Benjamin Spock', '**Dr. Benjamin McLane Spock** (1903–1998) was an American pediatrician and author whose landmark work, *The Common Sense Book of Baby and Child Care*, positioned him as one of the most influential child-rearing authorities of the twentieth century. Within the historical trajectory of the genital autonomy movement, Spock occupies a pivotal role as a medical authority whose public evolution on neonatal circumcision reflected and helped catalyze broader shifts in pediatric ethics and human rights discourse.

While early editions of his childcare manuals accepted routine circumcision as standard medical practice, Spock later reversed his stance, emerging as a vital champion for infant bodily integrity. His reconsideration of the procedure is extensively examined in historical retrospectives of intactivism, notably in *Origins of the Intactivist Movement: A Masculine Foundation* and *If You Prick Us: Masculinity and Circumcision Pain in the United States and Canada, 1960–2000*. By acknowledging infant pain, challenging the purported medical benefits of the surgery, and advising parents against non-therapeutic interventions, Spock leveraged his widespread cultural authority to bring skepticism of the practice into the mainstream domestic sphere.

Spock’s critique expanded the conversation beyond traditional medicalized justifications, prefiguring modern rights-based arguments regarding children''s bodily self-determination. His legacy remains a key touchstone within the legal and human rights literature surrounding genital autonomy, including analyses such as *Circumcision: Cultural-Legal Analysis*, *Circumcision: A Medical or a Human Rights Issue?*, and *Male Circumcision and the Rights of the Child*. Through his shift toward vocal opposition, Spock contributed significantly to reframing routine infant circumcision from a benign medical tradition into a serious human rights and child welfare concern.', 'Influential American pediatrician and author whose stance on routine infant circumcision shifted from standard approval to strong opposition later in his career.', 'champion', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
