INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-bf2732ed', 'person', 'Edward Wallerstein', 'Edward Wallerstein stands as an early champion and foundational intellectual figure within the modern movement for genital autonomy. Emerging as a pioneering voice in the late twentieth century, Wallerstein contributed significantly to the historical, statistical, and ideological groundwork that helped catalyze intactivism as a broader human rights struggle. His early critiques challenged institutionalized neonatal circumcision in the United States, providing a critical masculine perspective that scrutinized both medical orthodoxy and the societal rationales driving routine non-therapeutic genital cutting.

Wallerstein’s archival footprint reflects a multidimensional critique spanning demographic analysis, cultural history, and sociology. His inquiries closely examined American circumcision practices and social realities, analyzing demographic shifts in U.S. neonatal genital ratios to track intact versus circumcised populations. In the realm of cultural and historical scholarship, Wallerstein contributed substantively to the historiography of routine male circumcision, documenting its origins in Victorian medicalization and the masturbation taboos of the nineteenth century. In addition, his work carefully engaged with religious and ethical questions, articulating thoughtful inquiries into traditional Jewish circumcision practices and the moral imperative of bodily integrity.

Through these rigorous investigations, Wallerstein helped dismantle long-standing clinical and cultural justifications for non-therapeutic infant surgery. His scholarship armed subsequent generations of activists and researchers with demographic evidence and historical context, establishing his enduring legacy as a vital champion whose work helped legitimize the scholarly and human rights discourse of intactivism.', 'Author of ''Circumcision: An American Health Fallacy'' (1980), the intellectual foundation stone of modern intactivist literature.', 'champion', 1, 5, 'A+', 'Awarded an A+ grade for authoring the first comprehensive, scholarly investigation of the medical, cultural, and financial fallacies of American circumcision.', '', '', '', '')
