INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-464b6173', 'organization', 'The Hastings Center', 'The Hastings Center, founded in 1969, is an internationally recognized independent bioethics research institute dedicated to addressing fundamental ethical issues in healthcare, science, and public policy. Renowned for pioneering the formal study of bioethics, the Center produces landmark scholarship and hosts influential publications, most notably the *Hastings Center Report*, which serve as standard-bearing forums for examining the boundaries of medical necessity, human rights, and patient autonomy.

Within the archival record of the genital autonomy movement, The Hastings Center plays a notable role as an essential intellectual arena for critical discourse surrounding non-therapeutic pediatric genital interventions. Scholarship hosted and fostered under its auspices frequently engages the ethical, legal, and medical dimensions of child rights, bodily integrity, and public health policy. Key contributions in the archive reflect rigorous debates published or examined within the Center’s sphere, addressing foundational concerns such as moving toward an ethical consensus regarding medically unnecessary cutting, re-evaluating the physical and psychological harms of circumcision, and investigating contentious clinical questions regarding penile sensitivity and sexual experience.

Furthermore, discourse emerging from The Hastings Center has been instrumental in advancing an autonomy-based ethical framework that bridges discussions between female genital mutilation and non-therapeutic male circumcision. By subjecting prophylactic and cultural rationales for involuntary cutting to rigorous ethical scrutiny, the Center has helped center the rights and future self-determination of the child over parental and public health presumptions. Consequently, it remains an indispensable touchstone for scholars, legal analysts, and human rights advocates within the broader intactivist movement seeking to establish global standards for children''s genital autonomy.', 'A leading independent bioethics research institute whose scholarly publications have deeply shaped ethical discourse on pediatric bodily autonomy and routine circumcision.', 'notable', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
