INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-b9c5830d', 'person', 'P. C. Remondino', '**Peter Charles Remondino** (1846–1926) was a prominent late-nineteenth-century physician, author, and medical historian whose writings were instrumental in the medicalization and institutionalization of non-therapeutic neonatal circumcision in the United States. Within the historical scholarship of the genital autonomy and intactivism movements, Remondino is recognized as a pivotal historical figure whose promotional treatises helped shift the procedure from a cultural and religious rite into standard Western medical practice, a trajectory thoroughly scrutinized in analyses tracing the cultural and medical evolution of the surgery.

Remondino’s body of work is central to contemporary human rights analyses examining the historical justifications used to normalize infant genital modification. His literature frequently intersects with archival studies addressing the century-long denial of infant pain in medicine, illustrating how nineteenth-century clinical attitudes minimized pediatric surgical trauma to advance surgical interventions. Additionally, historical reviews engage with his writings on contemporaneous racial, sexual, and sociopolitical anxieties—reflected in works such as *Questions of the Day and Their Social Problems*—which movement scholars cite as evidence that non-therapeutic circumcision was codified not strictly on scientific merit, but alongside moral policing, racialized social theories, and psychosomatic concepts related to bodily suppression and armoring.

Within modern intactivist discourse, Remondino’s legacy serves as a primary subject of critical deconstruction. His early rationalizations were formally re-evaluated by movement critics at landmark symposiums, including the 1992 Austin Convention critiquing routine circumcision justifications. Today, researchers and genital autonomy advocates view Remondino’s medical arguments not as enduring clinical standards, but as historic artifacts of Victorian-era anxieties that entrenched non-consensual surgical practices within modern healthcare.', '19th-century American physician and author whose 1891 text strongly promoted routine medical circumcision.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
