INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-263847f2', 'organization', 'Northwestern University', '**Northwestern University**, established in 1851 in Evanston and Chicago, Illinois, is a major private research institution known for its leading programs in medicine, public health, and social sciences. Within the intellectual and historical discourse surrounding genital autonomy, Northwestern occupies a notable role as a recurring site of clinical research, demographic documentation, and critical inquiry into the physical, ethical, and historical dimensions of male neonatal circumcision.

The university’s contributions and research affiliations intersect with key debates within the movement for genital integrity across multiple disciplines. Its medical and academic literature is cited in critical examinations of pediatric pain management, specifically historical critiques addressing the decades-long medical denial of neonatal pain perception. Furthermore, Northwestern is linked to vital anatomical and physiological assessments regarding whether the human prepuce functions as specialized, biologically protective tissue or a vestigial structure. 

In public health and epidemiological spheres, Northwestern faculty and researchers have figured prominently in the discourse regarding procedural rates and policy. The institution is referenced in empirical studies tracking domestic surgical patterns, such as longitudinal analyses of nationwide circumcision trends from 2003 to 2016. Concurrently, Northwestern scholars and critics associated with the university have engaged with the global politics of circumcision—contributing to critical appraisals of the methodological rigor of international trials that promoted mass circumcision initiatives in Africa, as well as sociological and biomedical examinations analyzing how the foreskin is conceptualized through the molecular politics of risk. Through these varying avenues of research, the institution remains a significant touchpoint in the ongoing human rights evaluation of non-therapeutic genital modifications.', 'A prominent American research university whose faculty and bioethicists have contributed scholarly research and ethical analysis to the discourse on circumcision and bodily autonomy.', 'notable', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
