INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-3c691ad7', 'person', 'Bill Clinton', '**William Jefferson Clinton** (born August 19, 1946) is an American politician who served as the 42nd President of the United States from 1993 to 2001. Following his presidency, Clinton established philanthropic initiatives focusing on international development and global health. Within the scholarship of the genital autonomy and intactivism movements, his political legacy and post-presidential global health endeavors have drawn sustained scrutiny, positioning him as a prominent figure within debates surrounding state-sponsored public health policy and bodily integrity.

Clinton’s presence in intactivist discourse is primarily tied to international HIV/AIDS policy, federal funding allocations, and institutional oversight of medical interventions. Scholarly critiques addressing the global rollout of male circumcision campaigns in Sub-Saharan Africa—such as *Male Circumcision and HIV Prevention: Is There Really Enough of the Right Kind of Evidence?* and *The studies that launched a thousand snips*—examine the role of high-profile political leadership and institutional backing in promoting surgical interventions as health initiatives. 

Additionally, his administration and public health record are frequently analyzed by critics focusing on public expenditure and equal protection under the law. Works such as *SAVE A QUARTER-BILLION-DOLLARS ANNUALLY. DON’T FUND CIRCUMCISION!*, *Equal Justice?*, and *To Be or Not to Be: An Analytical Observation of Circumcision as a Technology of Control and Its Correlation with Institutional Sexual Violence Across Civilizations* engage with the broader policy, legal, and systemic dimensions of state-sanctioned bodily modifications, citing the political frameworks and funding mechanisms reinforced during and following his tenure.', '42nd President of the United States whose post-presidential foundation promoted voluntary medical male circumcision initiatives in Sub-Saharan Africa.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
