INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-345df1d3', 'person', 'Frederick Hodges', 'Frederick Hodges is an esteemed medical historian and recognized champion within the global movement for genital autonomy. His scholarship has provided the human rights movement with critical intellectual foundations by rigorously examining the medical, cultural, and socio-historical trajectories that led to the institutionalization of non-therapeutic neonatal circumcision in the Western world.

Hodges has contributed extensively to the historiography of modern circumcision practices. Through key treatises such as *The Masturbation Taboo and the Rise of Routine Male Circumcision: A Review of the Historiography*, he systematically dismantled the medical rationales of the procedure by linking its widespread adoption to Victorian-era moral panics and anti-masturbation anxieties. His archival investigations, including *A Short History of Circumcision in North America: In the Physicians'' Own Words* and *A Short History of the Institutionalization of Involuntary Sexual Mutilation in the United States*, serve as pivotal historical resources, highlighting the original rhetoric, biases, and flawed medical frameworks of the practitioners who first normalized the intervention.

Beyond his historical retrospectives, Hodges has engaged directly with modern clinical debate, critically evaluating contemporary justifications in assessments such as *Does science support infant circumcision?* His foundational insights have been documented across major literature in the field, including the prominent volume *Sexual Mutilations: A Human Tragedy*. Through his scholarly dedication to unearthing the true origins of infant genital alteration, Hodges remains an essential voice in advancing the understanding of bodily integrity and child protection.', 'Medical historian and researcher known for his foundational work on foreskin anatomy and the history of routine circumcision.', 'champion', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
