INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-13f93c2c', 'person', 'Peter Charles Remondino', '**Peter Charles Remondino** (1846–1926) was an American physician, surgeon, and author whose late-nineteenth-century writings played a foundational role in medicalizing and popularizing routine infant male circumcision in the English-speaking world. A prominent historical critic of the intact human foreskin, Remondino is widely recognized for his 1891 publication, *History of Circumcision*, which aggressively pathologized the natural prepuce. His advocacy framed non-therapeutic genital cutting as a prophylactic necessity, asserting that the procedure offered protection against a multitude of moral, systemic, and physical ailments, notably within historical medical debates concerning syphilis prevention between 1855 and 1914.

Within the archival record of the genital autonomy movement, Remondino is a central historical figure whose arguments serve as a primary subject of critical examination. Modern intactivist scholarship and bioethical literature routinely analyze his influence when deconstructing historical fallacies surrounding normal versus abnormal prepucial anatomy. His assertions are heavily cited in legal, human rights, and ethical inquiries into male circumcision—such as legal reviews questioning the institutionalization of non-therapeutic childhood procedures—to illustrate how Victorian-era medical dogmatism obscured ethical principles of bodily integrity and informed consent.

In contemporary studies documenting intactivism and anti-circumcision organizing in the United States, Remondino represents the genesis of routine neonatal cutting practices that genital autonomy advocates actively contest. Scholarly treatments examining the movement’s ideology, social media presence, and equity frameworks reference Remondino to historically ground the cultural and institutional momentum that intactivists counter, positioning his nineteenth-century medical crusade as a critical catalyst for the human rights movement that emerged to challenge it.', 'Nineteenth-century physician and author whose 1891 book served as a foundational medical text promoting routine male circumcision.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
