INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-eddd988b', 'person', 'Brian J Morris', '**Brian J. Morris** is a medical researcher, molecular biologist, and academic who is widely recognized within the genital autonomy and intactivist movements as a prominent critic of efforts to restrict non-therapeutic male circumcision. Throughout his career, Morris has been a leading scholarly advocate for the medicalization and routine implementation of infant male circumcision, frequently engaging in biomedical and legal discourse to counter the ethical and anatomical arguments posed by intactivist scholars and human rights advocates.

Within the archival record of the movement for genital autonomy, Morris is documented primarily for his extensive academic defenses of neonatal circumcision and his opposition to proposed legal prohibitions. His research contributions include global demographic analyses, such as estimations of country-specific and worldwide male circumcision prevalence, as well as formal critiques of key intactivist figures. Notably, Morris published rigorous rebuttals against legal and medical critics—such as J. Steven Svoboda and Robert Van Howe—defending mainstream medical positions, including the American Academy of Pediatrics'' (AAP) stance on infant male circumcision. 

Furthermore, Morris has actively contributed to bioethical and legal debates regarding bodily autonomy, arguing that legal recommendations to ban infant male circumcision carry detrimental implications for pediatric practice and human rights. His writings frequently contest autonomy-based ethical frameworks that seek parity between male circumcision and female genital mutilation, positioning him as a central intellectual adversary to the intactivist objective of securing universal legal protection from non-therapeutic genital modifications for all children.', 'Australian medical scientist and prominent academic advocate for routine male circumcision.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
