INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-391c1475', 'organization', 'Aalborg University', '**Aalborg University** (Danish: *Aalborg Universitet*), established in 1974 in Denmark, is a prominent public research institution recognized for its problem-based learning model and significant contributions to health sciences and epidemiology. Within the global movement for genital autonomy, the university occupies a notable role as an academic hub whose affiliated researchers have produced critical, peer-reviewed evidence evaluating the health outcomes, ethical considerations, and clinical justifications surrounding non-therapeutic male circumcision. Utilizing Denmark''s extensive national health data registries, researchers connected to the institution have generated pivotal epidemiological studies that inform modern intactivist scholarship and bioethical discourse.

The university’s contributions to the field are particularly distinguished by nationwide cohort studies investigating the long-term neurodevelopmental and physiological implications of early-life genital modifications. Affiliated scholars have published critical investigations into potential associations between ritual circumcision, early surgical interventions, and subsequent diagnoses of neurodevelopmental conditions such as autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). In addition, researchers associated with Aalborg University have rigorously scrutinized claims regarding circumcision as a preventative public health measure in developed nations, publishing key studies on the risk dynamics of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) and other sexually transmitted infections, as well as behavioral studies assessing sexual practices among circumcised populations. Through this sustained body of critical inquiry, Aalborg University has provided an evidentiary foundation that advocates and bioethicists routinely reference in defending the fundamental right of children to bodily integrity.', 'A Danish public university whose researchers have contributed significant epidemiological and health studies to the global discourse on non-therapeutic circumcision.', 'notable', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
