INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-c128a26e', 'person', 'John Taylor', '**John Taylor** is a prominent scholar, educator, and champion within the movement for genital autonomy, known for his critical contributions to the scientific, ethical, and human rights discourse surrounding infant and childhood circumcision. Within the broader intactivist movement, Taylor’s work has been instrumental in deconstructing cultural and medical rationales for non-therapeutic circumcision, emphasizing the functional significance of the intact human prepuce and the ethical necessity of bodily integrity.

Taylor has been an active participant in landmark international forums, notably contributing to the historic International Symposia on Circumcision. His presentations and leadership, prominently documented in the proceedings and program of the Second International Symposium on Circumcision, positioned him as an authoritative voice among bioethicists, clinicians, and human rights advocates. His recognized dedication to genital autonomy is further reflected in his formal inclusion in symposium speaker profiles and the movement''s associated Human Rights Awards.

In addition to conference leadership, Taylor’s scholarship spans both clinical inquiry and public advocacy literature. He has contributed to investigative works exploring penile anatomy, the physiology of the foreskin, and the clinical outcomes of surgical alteration, including specialized literature examining the prevalence of an excessive prepuce and the effects of distal circumcision on sexual function. His public education efforts—including contributions to critically focused works such as *Circumcision: Don''t Be Conned by the Pros*—have served to equip families, medical professionals, and advocates with evidence-based counterarguments to circumcision promotion, firmly establishing his legacy as a dedicated proponent of natural anatomy and human rights.', 'Human rights advocate and key figure in the international movement against non-consensual infant circumcision.', 'champion', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
