INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-4e0dde3c', 'organization', 'Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health', 'The Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health, established in 1916 in Baltimore, Maryland, is one of the world''s foremost academic public health institutions. Within the historical archives of the genital autonomy and intactivism movements, the institution occupies a prominent position as a primary source of epidemiological data and policy influence that has shaped the modern discourse surrounding male circumcision. Consequently, the school’s scientific output and policy engagement serve as central reference points for researchers and human rights advocates analyzing the biomedical justifications for non-therapeutic genital modifications.

Researchers affiliated with the Bloomberg School have significantly influenced global and domestic perspectives on male circumcision through extensive epidemiological and demographic investigations. Internationally, the institution has been heavily involved in field research across Sub-Saharan Africa, producing pivotal studies examining the transmission and anatomic sites of high-risk human papillomavirus (HPV) in Rakai, Uganda, as well as broader systematic reviews evaluating the procedure''s purported associations with female biomedical health outcomes. Beyond purely biological metrics, the institution''s scholarship has extended into behavioral and post-intervention studies, documenting evolving risk perceptions, health misconceptions, and social attitudes among women in regions such as Iringa, Tanzania. In the United States, the school has similarly served as a key authority on domestic neonatal circumcision patterns, publishing comprehensive data tracking procedural trends among newborn males.

Within the critical framework of the intactivist movement, the Bloomberg School is particularly scrutinized for its role in establishing international public health interventions. Archival records examine the school''s involvement in the socio-political construction of global "technical consultations" that positioned male circumcision as a recognized HIV prevention measure. Because its research has frequently provided the empirical foundation used by public health bodies to promote mass circumcision campaigns and neonatal procedures, the Bloomberg School remains a vital focal point in ongoing debates surrounding bodily integrity, ethical health governance, and human rights.', 'A premier public health institution whose faculty and research trials strongly influenced global and national policies advocating for male circumcision.', 'critic', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
