INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-c1301f88', 'person', 'Wilhelm Reich', 'Wilhelm Reich (1897–1957) was an Austrian psychoanalyst and psychiatrist whose pioneering theories regarding the somatic manifestations of trauma established early foundations for the understanding of infant bodily integrity. Known for developing body-oriented psychotherapy and character analysis, Reich introduced the concept of somatic "armoring"—the chronic physical and neuromuscular contractions developed by the body as a protective response to overwhelming stress, fear, and pain. Through his work establishing the Orgonomic Infant Research Center and publications such as *Children of the Future* and clinical observations detailing armoring in newborn infants, Reich provided some of the earliest psychoanalytic documentations of how early traumatic interventions physically and emotionally mark the developing child.

Within the human rights movement for genital autonomy, Reich is recognized as an early intellectual champion whose models of trauma and somatic defense inform intactivist critique. His work serves as a cornerstone for evaluating the neurodevelopmental and emotional consequences of early surgery, appearing across foundational movement literature including *The Psychological Impact of Circumcision* and *Sexual Mutilations: A Human Tragedy*. Reichian somatic psychology is likewise central to clinical investigations into adult healing, such as *The Impact of Neonatal Circumcision: Implications for Doctors of Men’s Experiences In Regressive Therapy*, which examine how preverbal trauma is stored within the body. Through his early defense of the natural emotional and physical vitality of children, Reich remains a significant historical influence in articulating the psychological imperative to protect infants from non-therapeutic genital cutting.', 'Austrian psychoanalyst and pioneer of somatic psychology who identified routine infant circumcision as a traumatic violation of bodily autonomy and sexual integrity.', 'champion', 1, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
