INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('entity-fa696ffb', 'person', 'Sydney Gellis', '**Sydney S. Gellis, M.D.** (1914–2005) was a prominent American pediatrician, medical educator, and longtime editor whose authoritative presence in mainstream medicine established him as an essential early champion of children''s right to genital autonomy. Widely known for his tenure as the chairman of pediatrics at Tufts University School of Medicine and as the editor-in-chief of the *Year Book of Pediatrics*, Gellis brought rigorous clinical skepticism to bear on common pediatric surgical practices, emerging as one of the earliest prominent medical voices to challenge routine neonatal circumcision in North America.

Within the literature and historical record of the intactivist movement, Gellis is recognized for systematically dismantling clinical rationales that perpetuated non-therapeutic circumcision. His scholarship directly targeted unsubstantiated prophylactic claims, most notably by scrutinizing assertions regarding malignancy in assessments such as *Does Circumcision Prevent Cancer?* and dissecting cultural dogmas examined in broader critiques like *From Medicine to Myth*. His critical insights helped lay the groundwork for public and professional education, echoing through popular advocacy literature such as *Circumcision: Don’t Be Conned by the Pros* and aligning with the clinical evidence later cited when major professional bodies, such as the Canadian Paediatric Society, formally upheld policies against routine infant circumcision. By applying scientific objectivity to an entrenched cultural custom, Gellis served as a vital bridge between mainstream clinical pediatrics and the broader movement for bodily integrity.', 'Prominent American pediatrician and medical journal editor who challenged routine neonatal circumcision in medical literature.', 'champion', 0, 3.0, 'C', '', '', '', '', '')
