INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('hugh-c-thompson', 'person', 'Hugh C. Thompson Jr., M.D.', '### Historical Significance

Dr. Hugh C. Thompson Jr. was an American pediatrician and clinical leader who served as Chairman of the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) Committee on Fetus and Newborn during the 1970s. In that capacity, Dr. Thompson presided over one of the most critical inflection points in the institutional history of American pediatric medicine: the issuance of the landmark 1975 AAP policy statement concluding that there are **no valid medical indications for routine circumcision in the neonatal period**.

### The 1975 Breakthrough

Throughout the mid-20th century, American hospitals operated on a default conveyor belt that circumcised upwards of 80% to 90% of newborn boys without medical necessity. In 1971, Dr. Thompson and an ad-hoc committee began examining the empirical literature, discovering that decades of routine neonatal surgery rested on cultural inertia, Victorian anxieties, and institutional habit rather than rigorous clinical evidence.

In 1975, the Committee on Fetus and Newborn published its formal findings in *Pediatrics*. The report represented a courageous institutional departure: for the first time, the preeminent pediatric body in the United States officially advised against routine cutting. The document stated unequivocally:

> *''There are no valid medical indications for circumcision in the neonatal period.''*

### Archival Legacy

Dr. Thompson''s personal papers, committee correspondence, and draft deliberations are preserved in the **Tim Hammond Papers (UMass MS 1205, Box 7, Folder 1)**. These archival records reveal the immense institutional pushback Thompson and his colleagues faced from obstetricians and pro-circumcision pediatricians who feared the loss of procedural revenue and cultural legitimacy. Although the AAP would later compromise its stance in 1989 and 2012 under chairs like Dr. Edgar Schoen and Dr. Douglas Diekema, Thompson''s 1975 ruling remains a touchstone of objective clinical bioethics.', 'Chair of the AAP Committee on Fetus and Newborn whose landmark 1975 policy declared no valid medical indications exist for routine infant circumcision.', 'Pediatrician / Historical Whistleblower', 1, 4.2, 'B+', 'Presided over the historic 1975 AAP policy statement that broke ranks with American hospital routine, though the academy later backtracked under cultural pressure.', '1940s–1980s', 'American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), AAP Committee on Fetus and Newborn, University of Arizona College of Medicine', 'Neonatal pediatrics, pediatric ethics, institutional circumcision policy review', '1971 AAP Ad Hoc Task Force on Circumcision; 1975 Author and Chair of the historic AAP policy statement declaring ''There are no valid medical indications for circumcision in the neonatal period''; archive preserved in UMass MS 1205 Box 7.')
