INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('brian-buckler', 'person', 'Brian Buckler, M.D.', '### Scholarly Overview

Dr. Brian Buckler, M.D., is an American physician and bioethical researcher whose scholarship has exposed the structural biases and hidden financial conflicts of interest that distort medical literature concerning non-therapeutic infant circumcision. His investigative analyses, published in prestigious journals such as the *Journal of Medical Ethics*, have fundamentally altered the academic conversation surrounding institutional pediatric guidance.

### Exposing Institutional Conflicts of Interest

In a series of landmark papers (2024–2025), Dr. Buckler and his co-authors meticulously audited the memberships and drafting processes of major policy statements issued by the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). Buckler demonstrated that these panels repeatedly failed to disclose profound conflicts of interest—including patent holdings on surgical clamp devices, financial dependence on procedural billing codes, and explicit personal affiliations with circumcision promotion organizations.

Furthermore, Buckler’s epidemiological analyses revealed how pro-circumcision task forces selectively cited flawed observational data while ignoring contrary prospective European studies. His work demonstrated that the claimed ''benefits'' (such as marginal reductions in infant urinary tract infections) required hundreds of healthy newborns to undergo irreversible surgery to theoretically prevent a single treatable infection—a risk-benefit ratio that violates fundamental bioethical tenets.

### Clinical and Legal Impact

Dr. Buckler''s forensic critiques have been entered into the record in ongoing federal civil rights lawsuits (such as *Hadachek v. State of Oregon*) and have prompted formal editorial inquiries and retraction reviews across international biomedical publications. His work represents a modern renaissance in clinical accountability.', 'Lead author of landmark 2024–2025 Journal of Medical Ethics investigations exposing conflicts of interest and flawed methodologies in pro-circumcision pediatric task forces.', 'Physician / Bioethics Researcher', 1, 4.8, 'A', 'A rigorous clinical scholar holding institutional medicine accountable to empirical scrutiny and transparent conflict-of-interest standards.', '2010s–Present', 'Journal of Medical Ethics, Global Pediatric Health Consortium, Genital Autonomy Legal Defense', 'Medical ethics, epidemiological critique, conflict-of-interest disclosures, procedural bias in pediatric task forces', '2024 Publication in Journal of Medical Ethics detailing undisclosed financial ties in circumcision advocacy; 2025 Retraction petitions and methodological audits of CDC and AAP claims.')
