INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('canadian-foreskin-awareness-project', 'organization', 'Canadian Foreskin Awareness Project (CAN-FAP)', '### History and Mission

The **Canadian Foreskin Awareness Project (CAN-FAP)** is Canada’s leading grassroots organization dedicated to raising public consciousness about the vital functions of the human prepuce and ending routine infant circumcision across the provinces. Founded in Vancouver by activist Glen Callender in 2010, CAN-FAP distinguished itself through innovative, body-positive, and joyful public messaging.

### Foreskin Pride and Media Campaigns

CAN-FAP made international headlines by organizing the world''s first annual **Foreskin Pride** marches and educational rallies in Vancouver. Unlike purely solemn protests, Callender’s approach combined serious legal and biological critiques with vibrant artistic installations, colorful banners, and an open celebration of natural sexual anatomy.

### Policy Advocacy in Canada

CAN-FAP has actively engaged with the Canadian Paediatric Society (CPS), health ministries, and provincial medical associations, demanding the complete de-insuring and legal prohibition of non-therapeutic cosmetic surgery on minors. The organization’s work has contributed significantly to the steep decline of circumcision in Canada, where rates have dropped from over 60% in the 1970s to below 20% today.', 'Canada''s premier grassroots intactivist organization, founded by Glen Callender to celebrate intact anatomy and dismantle cultural circumcision myths.', 'Educational & Cultural Advocacy Group', 0, 4.7, 'A', 'Creative, body-positive Canadian intactivism pairing sharp human rights critiques with celebratory public education.', '2010–Present', 'Glen Callender, Intact America, Canadian Paediatric Society, GAIN', 'Body-positive sex education, cultural awareness, Foreskin Pride rallies, public media engagement', 'Organization of the world''s first ''Foreskin Pride'' rally in Vancouver; Co-organizer of multiple Canadian hospital and university awareness campaigns.')
