INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('michelle-geren', 'person', 'Michelle Geren, Ph.D.', '### Academic Contributions

Dr. Michelle Geren is an epidemiologist and statistical methodologist whose critical evaluations of global health intervention data have fundamentally challenged the scientific consensus surrounding Voluntary Medical Male Circumcision (VMMC) campaigns in Sub-Saharan Africa.

### Auditing the African HIV Trials

In the mid-2000s, three randomized controlled trials conducted in South Africa, Uganda, and Kenya claimed that adult circumcision reduced female-to-male HIV transmission by ''60%''. These trials led the WHO and UNAIDS to launch massive multi-billion-dollar circumcision programs across the African continent, and were cited by the American Academy of Pediatrics to justify infant circumcision in the United States.

Dr. Geren''s forensic re-analysis demonstrated that the headline ''60%'' figure was a heavily distorted *relative risk reduction*, masking an extraordinarily modest *absolute risk reduction* of barely 1.3% (from ~2.5% in the control group to ~1.2% in the surgical group). Furthermore, Geren identified fatal methodological flaws across all three trials, including:
1. Premature trial termination before long-term infection rates equalized;
2. Severe attrition bias (participants lost to follow-up);
3. Uncontrolled behavioral compensation and condom distribution differences.

Her work dismantled the pseudo-scientific pretense that infant circumcision functions as a ''surgical vaccine'' against sexually transmitted infections.', 'Epidemiological scholar associated with Pasteur Institute analyses who exposed the severe statistical confounding in African adult circumcision HIV trials.', 'Epidemiologist / Statistical Auditor', 0, 4.6, 'A', 'A rigorous biostatistician whose mathematical deconstruction pierced the promotional claims of global VMMC campaigns.', '2000s–Present', 'Pasteur Institute Research Affiliates, Global Health Epidemiology Forum', 'Epidemiological trial methodology, attrition bias, relative vs. absolute risk reporting, African VMMC evaluation', 'Statistical audit of the Orange Farm, Rakai, and Kisumu HIV trials; Published analyses demonstrating that absolute risk reduction was under 1.3%.')
