INSERT INTO entities (id, type, name, description, tagline, role, featured, rating_score, rating_grade, rating_quote, years_active, affiliations, core_focus, key_milestones)
VALUES ('michigan-state-university-special-collections', 'organization', 'Michigan State University Special Collections', '### Archival Importance

The **Michigan State University Libraries Special Collections** in East Lansing, Michigan, houses one of the world''s most significant institutional archives of counter-cultural, gender, and men''s studies history: the **Changing Men Collections (MS 36674694)**.

### Preserving the Intactivist Paper Trail

During the 1980s and 1990s, when mainstream medical journals were largely hostile or indifferent to challenges against routine circumcision, activists and dissenting physicians communicated through specialized periodicals such as *Changing Men: Issues in Gender, Sex, and Politics*, NOCIRC bulletins, and grassroots newsletters. 

MSU Special Collections meticulously cataloged and preserved this fragile paper trail, including hospital administrative clipping dossiers from 1991 to 2000, early legal memos, and promotional hospital literature from the peak of American hospital routines. This collection provides historians and researchers with irreplaceable evidence of how the intactivist movement organized, articulated its bioethical principles, and gradually shifted public opinion.', 'Major university archival repository preserving the Changing Men Collections (MS 36674694) documenting 40 years of circumcision and gender history.', 'Academic Archival Institution', 0, 4.8, 'A', 'An invaluable academic haven preserving the ephemeral history, grassroots newsletters, and early debate of the American intactivist movement.', '1970s–Present', 'Michigan State University Libraries, Changing Men Collections, UMass Amherst Special Collections', 'Archival preservation, men''s movement history, gender studies, medicalization ephemera', 'Acquisition and preservation of the MS 36674694 Changing Men dossiers containing original 1980s–1990s intactivist bulletins, brochures, and hospital clipping archives.')
